b/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416433 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416433 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416433 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416433 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416433 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416433 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e35daaf4-8a57-5507-af06-07e8ca410d81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416434 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416434 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416434 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416434 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416434 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416434 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416434 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416434 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416434 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd23a28f-9c20-551f-9dfa-96e071ee475a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416435 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416435 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416435 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416435 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+3z=1 \\\\  & y-2z=-1 \\\\  & z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ad315bd-aaaa-532d-a173-690fb5924119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416435 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=-5 \\\\  & y=3 \\\\  & z=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=-5$, $y=3$, and $z=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416436 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416436 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416436 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416436 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y+5z=1 \\\\ -x+4y-3z=-1 \\\\ 6y+12z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ba94d2c-82a9-5b70-84fa-d478e1834095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416436 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416437 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416437 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416437 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z=6 \\\\ x+y+z=10 \\\\ -4x-8y-4z=-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "751f8e53-b86d-5abb-abe8-9ed7db35005a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416437 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416438 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416438 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416438 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416438 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+6y-9z=1 \\\\ 4x+24y-36z=4 \\\\ 2x+12y-18z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "936fd617-fd7c-5014-a3dd-661b242ff833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416438 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 24 & -36 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 24 & -36 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+6y-9z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-6y+9z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-6y+9z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
